MODELING DIELECTRICS IN PURE DATA (Pd)
FULL FRAMEWORK
=====================================================================

OVERVIEW
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Pure Data can model dielectrics because:
  - Pd handles continuous-time signals (E-fields, D-fields).
  - Pd supports stateful processes (polarization memory).
  - Pd supports linear and nonlinear operators (κ, χ_e, hysteresis).
  - Pd supports modular abstractions (each polarization mechanism).
  - Pd supports feedback loops (ferroelectric behavior, relaxation).

We treat Pd signals as "fields" and Pd objects as "operators."

1. CORE VARIABLES AS Pd SIGNALS
---------------------------------------------------------------------
E(t)      Electric field            → audio-rate signal
P(t)      Polarization              → audio-rate signal with memory
D(t)      Displacement field        → derived signal
κ         Relative permittivity     → control-rate parameter
χ_e       Susceptibility            → control-rate parameter
ε₀        Vacuum permittivity       → constant (control-rate)
ε         Absolute permittivity     → control-rate parameter

Voltage V(t) and charge Q(t) can also be Pd signals.

2. BASIC LINEAR DIELECTRIC MODEL IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Given:
  P(t) = ε₀ * (κ - 1) * E(t)
  D(t) = ε₀ * E(t) + P(t)
       = κ * ε₀ * E(t)

Pd implementation:
  [E~] →[*~ κ]→[*~ ε₀]→[D~]

Polarization path:
  [E~]→[*~ (κ - 1)]→[*~ ε₀]→[P~]

These can be abstractions:
  dielectric.pd
  polarization.pd
  displacement.pd

3. FREQUENCY-DEPENDENT PERMITTIVITY IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Real materials have:
  ε(ω) = ε'(ω) - j ε''(ω)

Pd implementation:
  - Use filters to model relaxation.
  - Use complex operators via paired signals (real/imag).

Debye relaxation:
  P(t) + τ dP/dt = ε₀(κ - 1)E(t)

Pd patch:
  [E~]
    |
  [lop~ cutoff = 1/τ] → gives P(t)

Lorentz oscillator:
  d²P/dt² + γ dP/dt + ω₀² P = α E

Pd patch:
  [E~]→[bp~ ω₀ γ]→[*~ α]→[P~]

4. SPACE-CHARGE POLARIZATION IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Space-charge = slow accumulation at boundaries.

Pd implementation:
  - Use integrators.
  - Use very low-frequency filters.

Patch:
  [E~]→[lop~ 0.1 Hz]→[P_sc~]

5. FERROELECTRIC (NONLINEAR) MODEL IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Ferroelectrics have hysteresis:
  P = P_s * tanh(E/E_c)

Pd implementation:
  [E~]→[/~ E_c]→[tanh~]→[*~ P_s]→[P~]

Add memory via feedback:
  [P~]→[delwrite~]→[delread~]→[mix]→[P~]

6. CAPACITOR MODEL IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Capacitance:
  C = κ ε₀ (A/d)

Voltage-charge relation:
  Q(t) = C * V(t)

Pd patch:
  [V~]→[*~ C]→[Q~]

Energy:
  U(t) = (1/2) C V(t)²

Pd patch:
  [V~]→[*~ V]→[*~ (C/2)]→[U~]

7. FULL DIELECTRIC BLOCK IN Pd
---------------------------------------------------------------------

             +------------------------------+
             |        DIELECTRIC BLOCK      |
[E~]-------->| polarization mechanisms       |
             |   electronic: [bp~]          |
             |   ionic:      [bp~]          |
             |   dipolar:    [lop~]         |
             |   space-charge:[lop~ slow]   |
             +------------------------------+
                       |
                       v
                [sum of P~]
                       |
                       v
             +------------------------------+
             |   D = ε₀E + P                |
             +------------------------------+
                       |
                       v
             +------------------------------+
             |   capacitor model            |
             |   Q = C V                    |
             |   U = 1/2 C V²               |
             +------------------------------+

8. WHAT CAN DO IN Pd WITH THIS MODEL
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- Simulate dielectric relaxation.
- Visualize polarization vs field.
- Model capacitor charging with real dielectric physics.
- Build ferroelectric hysteresis loops.
- Create audio-rate "materials" with dielectric-like responses.
- Build modular dielectric abstractions for reuse.

